'''East Cape''' is a New Zealand Parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], that existed from 1978 to 1993, and from 2026 onwards.

==Population centres==
The 1977 electoral redistribution was the most overtly political since the Representation Commission had been established through an amendment to the ''Representation Act'' in 1886, initiated by [[Robert Muldoon|Muldoon]]'s [[Third National Government of New Zealand|National Government]].{{sfn|McRobie |1989 |pp=8–9, 51, 119}} As part of the [[1976 New Zealand census|1976 census]], a large number of people failed to fill out an electoral re-registration card, and census staff had not been given the authority to insist on the card being completed. This had little practical effect for people on the general roll, but it transferred [[Māori people|Māori]] to the general roll if the card was not handed in. Together with a northward shift of New Zealand's population, this resulted in five new electorates having to be created in the upper part of the [[North Island]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=119}} The electoral redistribution was very disruptive, and 22 electorates were abolished, while 27 electorates were newly created (including East Cape) or re-established. These changes came into effect for the {{NZ election link|1978}}.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=115–120}}

The East Cape electorate was made up of areas that previously belonged to {{NZ electorate link|Gisborne}} (which moved south) and {{NZ electorate link|Bay of Plenty}} (which was abolished).{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=114, 118}} Settlements in the original electorate included [[Whakatāne]], [[Ōpōtiki]], [[Taneatua]], [[Te Kaha]], [[Tokomaru Bay]], [[Tolaga Bay]], and [[Matawai, New Zealand|Matawai]]. The electorate stopped just short of the city of [[Gisborne, New Zealand|Gisborne]], but included the suburb of [[Kaiti, New Zealand|Kaiti]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=118}}

Subsequent boundary changes through the 1983 electoral redistribution drew the electorate further from Gisborne and included [[Kawerau]] and [[Murupara]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=118, 122}}

In [[1993 New Zealand general election|1993]], the East Cape electorate was abolished and most of its area was included in the [[Eastern Bay of Plenty (New Zealand electorate)|Eastern Bay of Plenty]] electorate, with the portion from Hicks Bay to the south of Tokomaru Bay included in the {{NZ electorate link|Mahia}} electorate.

==History==
The East Cape electorate was first won by [[Duncan MacIntyre (New Zealand politician)|Duncan MacIntyre]] of the National Party in 1978, who had previously been the representative for the Bay of Plenty electorate (1975–78).{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=215}} When he retired in {{NZ election link year|1984}},{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=215}} the seat was won by Labour's [[Anne Fraser]] with a majority of around 800 votes.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=198}} She held the seat at the subsequent {{NZ election link|1987}} by less than 240 votes, and in 1989, her name officially reverted to her maiden name Anne Collins after she had separated from her husband.<ref>{{cite web|title=Card Index: Collins, Anne|url=https://kura.aucklandlibraries.govt.nz/digital/collection/indexcards/id/275306|date=17 June 1989|page=5|publisher=New Zealand Herald |access-date=11 July 2024}}</ref>

[[Tony Ryall]] from the National Party won the seat in the 1990 general election landslide to the National Party.

As part of the Representation Commission Boundary Review in 2025, it was announced that the East Cape electorate would be re-introduced after public consultation for the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 election]], while retaining the same boundaries of the prior [[East Coast (New Zealand electorate)|East Coast]] electorate.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Boundary Review 2025 {{!}} Electorate maps |url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/historical-events/boundary-review-2025/electorate-maps |access-date=22 April 2026 |website=elections.nz}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=8 August 2025 |title=Electorate boundaries finalised |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/electorate-boundaries-finalised/// |work=Elections NZ}}</ref>

